# Era 1, The Exchange: the frost list at the road post (e1.s07, fate f.e1_sallis, Josh Brack decides), the
# census table (e1.s08, the hinge), Osby fair and the barn door (e1.s09) and the new table (e1.s10). Story
# lead, W2-F, 2026-10-22. DRAFT, every line awaiting the author.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- e1.s07, the frost list
@node e1.frost
place: q_post
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
nar: Three days after the supper, an ordinary Tuesday. The first frost. The road post at seven in the morning, white, and the pole furred with it, and the hill behind gone sharp the way it does on a frost, so you can see every wall on it.
nar: The list is pinned to the post board on a nail. Names in Edwin Mott's hand, nice and round. HANDS, SEASONAL, NOT RESIDENT. Twenty-odd names and their households. The hands are in the road below the pole, with their bundles, because they were told to be.
nar: Josh Brack has the list off the nail and is reading it out. He does it the same every year, so it's not like anything. It's like reading the weather.
line: JOSH: Pruett. Pruett and four. Up you go, Pruett.
line: JOSH: Tesk. The old man's gone up already, has he. Right.
line: JOSH: Sallis. Arn. Wife and the lad.
line: ARN: I've work at Orme's till the potatoes are up.
line: JOSH: Have you a paper says so?
line: ARN: Mr Orme said.
line: JOSH: Mr Orme says a lot of things in August.
line: KETTI: The lad's got a cough. Can we not go up Monday?
line: JOSH: I don't set the frost, missus.
nar: Davin Sallis, eight, is standing behind his mother's skirt. He coughs into it, a wet cough that goes on too long, and she puts her hand on his head without looking.
alt: e1.frost.abel ? q_class == inspector
go: e1.frost.josh

@node e1.frost.abel
place: q_post
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
nar: Abel Furrowe has come down the road in his trap to see the hands go up, which he does every year, because they are the exchange's hands as much as anybody's. He sits on the box with his hat on and the reins loose.
voice: sum: The loft over the horse takes two at a pinch. One's spoken for. {dc: 2}
opt: "Say it: Josh. I've room over the horse for Sallis till the potatoes. Put him down as with me." -> e1.frost.decide {do: set(said.e1_abel_loft)}
opt: "Sit on the box and watch them go up." -> e1.frost.decide

@node e1.frost.josh
place: q_post
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
nar: George Orme is coming along the road in his trap with Haig, out to the potato ground, and pulls up behind the hands because he can't get past.
voice: ballast: Read it. Read it and they go. It's not your list. {dc: 2}
opt: "Say it: Mr Orme. Is Sallis yours till the potatoes or isn't he? Say it and I'll write him down." -> e1.frost.decide {do: set(said.e1_josh_carter)}
opt: "Pin the list back on the nail. Say it: Up you go, Sallis." -> e1.frost.decide

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- fate f.e1_sallis: Josh decides
@node e1.frost.decide
cost: 0
alt: e1.frost.kept ? has(said.e1_abel_loft) | has(said.e1_josh_carter)
go: e1.frost.sent

@node e1.frost.kept
place: q_post
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
do: set(e1.sallis_kept)
line: JOSH: With you, Mr Furrowe. {when: has(said.e1_abel_loft)}
line: ABEL: With me. {when: has(said.e1_abel_loft)}
nar: Josh looks at Abel for a long moment, at the hat and the trap and the name painted on the trap's side, and then he licks his pencil and writes something in the margin of Mott's list. {when: has(said.e1_abel_loft)}
line: GEORGE: Well. I did say. Two-thirds the carters' rate, mind. He's not a carter. {when: has(said.e1_josh_carter)}
line: JOSH: Two-thirds. I'll write it. Sallis, Arn, carter to Orme. {when: has(said.e1_josh_carter)}
nar: Haig leans over and says something to George about Gedge's father, who has carted for the mill for twenty years at the full rate. George says he knows. {when: has(said.e1_josh_carter)}
line: JOSH: Sallis. Stand out of the road, then. You're kept.
nar: Arn doesn't move for a second, as if he hasn't heard it right. Then Ketti takes him by the sleeve and takes Davin by the hand and walks them both to the side of the road, onto the verge, and they stand there in the frost while the others go past them up the hill road, and the others don't look at them, and they don't look at the others.
line: KETTI: Say thank you.
line: ARN: Thank you, Mr Brack.
line: JOSH: It's not me.
go: e1.frost.up

@node e1.frost.sent
place: q_post
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
do: set(e1.sallis_sent); fate(f.e1_sallis, ketti)
line: JOSH: Up you go, Sallis.
nar: Arn picks up the big bundle, and Ketti the small one, and Davin a tin kettle on a string. They go through under the pole when Will lifts it, and start up the hill road behind the Pruetts.
go: e1.frost.up

@node e1.frost.up
place: q_hill
cost: 0
camera: wide
nar: From the post, held: the hands going up the hill road in a line, with their bundles on their backs, the way they came down in August, only slower, because it's uphill and it's cold. The frost is going off the road where the sun touches it and staying where it doesn't. Josh stands at the pole with the list.
nar: Halfway up, Ketti Sallis stops to cough, bent over with her hand on her knee, and Arn waits for her with the bundle, and Davin waits with the kettle, and then they go on. {when: has(e1.sallis_sent)}
nar: They go over the shoulder of the hill one at a time, and then there's only the road.
voice: undertow: That winter, on the hill. You won't hear about it till you're old. {dc: 4, when: has(e1.sallis_sent)}
go: e1.post.will

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- e1.s08, the census table (the hinge)
@node e1.census
place: q1_census
speaker: EDWIN
cost: 0
nar: The registrar's front counter, the Friday. Census day for the new rule: everybody with a fixed abode comes in and is written down. The queue goes out of the door and along the front of the building and round the corner, and it's raining, and the ones outside hold their coats over their heads.
nar: Edwin Mott at the counter with the big book. Walter beside him with the copy slips, writing his sevens with no bar through them, which his father has told him about twice.
nar: Ilo in the queue with Bet, and Anni asleep on Bet's hip with her thumb in. Ilo has a clean shirt on that he borrowed. He has the crooked spoon in his pocket and keeps touching it.
alt: e1.census.door ? q_class == militia
opt: "Go and stand in the doorway, with your hat in your hands." -> e1.census.door {do: set(e1.per_af)}
opt: "Stay at home and send Mott a note." -> e1.census.letter {do: set(e1.per_letter)}

@node e1.census.door
place: q1_census
speaker: EDWIN
cost: 0
do: set(e1.per_af)
nar: Abel Furrowe is in the doorway, just inside out of the rain, with his hat in his hands, the way a man stands at the back of a church he doesn't go to. He isn't in the queue. He doesn't need to be.
nar: Josh Brack is at the end of the counter for the hands' entries, with the frost list, to cross off whoever turns up resident after all. {when: q_class == militia}
go: e1.census.name

@node e1.census.letter
place: q1_census
speaker: EDWIN
cost: 0
nar: There's a note on the counter by Edwin's elbow, folded, with a good seal on it, and Edwin keeps it under his hand and doesn't open it until it's wanted.
go: e1.census.name

@node e1.census.name
place: q1_census
speaker: EDWIN
cost: 0
line: EDWIN: Next. Name.
line: ILO: Ilo.
line: EDWIN: Surname.
line: ILO: Ilo.
line: EDWIN: Your other name. Your father's name.
line: ILO: My father was Ilo as well.
nar: Somebody behind them in the queue laughs, and then stops.
line: EDWIN: I need a surname. The column's for a surname.
line: ILO: We don't have them up there. You're your father's son, that's all. You're Ilo who's Ilo's.
line: EDWIN: Well, you're down here now. Where are you resident.
line: ILO: The barns at Haldane's. From Sunday.
nar: Edwin looks past him, at the door, where Mr Furrowe is standing with his hat. {when: has(e1.per_af) & !has(e1.per_letter)}
nar: Edwin takes the note out from under his hand and reads it, and folds it up again. {when: has(e1.per_letter)}
nar: Edwin dips the pen. His pen is over the column, and Ilo can see it there, waiting, and the queue is waiting, and the rain is coming in round the door.
line: ILO: Put Haldane.
line: EDWIN: Haldane.
line: ILO: After the farm. Put Haldane.
nar: Edwin writes it. He strikes through ILO in the first column and writes HALDANE, I., and then after it, small, the way he'd note a source: after the farm. Walter copies it onto a slip and blots it and pushes it across the counter.
do: set(e1.named)
voice: sum: You saw him decide it. He decided it before the pen came down, so it's his name. That's what you'll say. {dc: 3}
go: e1.census.bet

@node e1.census.bet
place: q1_census
speaker: EDWIN
cost: 0
line: EDWIN: Wife.
line: BET: Bet. Elizabeth. Carrow, it was.
line: EDWIN: Married when.
line: BET: Sunday.
line: EDWIN: The child.
line: BET: Anni.
line: EDWIN: Born.
line: BET: Two years at Lammas.
line: EDWIN: Father.
nar: Bet doesn't answer. She hitches Anni up her hip. She doesn't look at the door. She looks at the counter, at the grain of the wood. {when: has(e1.per_af)}
nar: Bet doesn't answer. She hitches Anni up her hip. Edwin looks at the note again. {when: has(e1.per_letter)}
nar: Edwin rules a line through the father column with his ruler, neat, all the way across. Then he takes a pencil out from behind his ear, not the pen, and writes in the margin beside it: per A.F. {when: has(e1.per_af)}
nar: Edwin rules a line through the father column with his ruler, neat, all the way across. Then he takes a pencil out from behind his ear, not the pen, and writes in the margin beside it: per letter, A.F. {when: has(e1.per_letter)}
line: EDWIN: Next.
nar: In the doorway, Abel puts his hat back on and goes out into the rain. {when: has(e1.per_af)}
nar: Outside, Ilo unfolds the slip and holds it so the rain won't get it and looks at it a long time, though he can't read it. Bet reads it to him. Then he folds it small and puts it in the inside pocket of the borrowed shirt.
line: ILO: Haldane. Haldane. Haldane.
line: BET: You'll get used to it.
line: ILO: I'll have to, won't I. It's on a paper.
go: e1.wedding

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- e1.s09, Osby fair and the barn door
@node e1.osby
place: q1_osby
cost: 0
camera: wide
nar: Osby fair, the Sunday after the wedding, which was quick and had six people at it. The crockery stall at the end of the row under a striped awning, with bowls stacked up in towers.
nar: Bet has the wedding money in a handkerchief, from Abel, in an envelope with nothing written on it. She picks up the bowls one at a time and taps each one with her nail and listens. She buys five, matched, the blue rim.
line: ILO: Five. There's three of us.
line: BET: My mam said buy five.
line: ILO: Your mam's not paying for them.
line: BET: My mam said buy five, Ilo.
nar: He carries them home in the basket on his arm, all the way, with Anni on his shoulders holding his ears.
go: e1.door

@node e1.door
place: q1_barns
cost: 0
camera: wide
nar: The barns at Haldane's, the Monday. Old Carrow comes over from the Osby road with a crowbar, and he and Ilo take the big door off the end barn. It comes off the pins with a shriek. It's heavier than either of them thought, and they have to put it down twice crossing the yard, and the second time Carrow sits down on it and laughs till he coughs.
line: CARROW: You're marrying my girl and you're stealing a door off Furrowe.
line: ILO: It's my door. I rent it.
line: CARROW: Since Sunday.
line: ILO: Since Sunday.
nar: Bet stands in the kitchen doorway with Anni and the basket of bowls and doesn't help, and tells them they're doing it wrong. The hinge pins stay in the barn wall, with nothing on them.
nar: There's a blue thread tied through the hinge hole in the door, knotted twice, where nobody would tie one. Ilo looks at it, and leaves it. {when: prior(q.fact.corner)}
do: set(e1.door_down)
go: e1.table

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- e1.s10, the narrow lens: the new table
@node e1.table
place: q_haldane
cost: 0
camera: near
nar: The Haldane kitchen. The table, new, made of a barn door on four legs, with the hinge marks still in it where Ilo couldn't plane them out. On one of the legs, low down, where you'd only see it if you were a child, Ilo has carved a goat with its head turned back over its shoulder, the way they carve them on the hill.
nar: Three bowls on the table. Three places. Bread, and a little dripping, and an apple for Anni cut small.
nar: Two bowls on the shelf over the stove, turned over, so the dust won't get in them. For whoever comes.
nar: Ilo puts the crooked spoon by Anni's bowl. She picks it up and bangs the table with it. She does it again, and he laughs.
voice: dark: You know this table. {dc: 2}
go: e1.hill
